{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfnjNyK6rmg5AQkeDwcKZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These are the issues I hit, most important first. I also made one mistake of my own, in the first item.\n\n1. **`sauce_pools` on Solana returned too much, and I missed the fix.** I expected a usable result for USDC/SOL. I got a 68k-character response that overflowed the tool limit and was dumped to a file, so I had to slice it with `jq`. The tool has a `limit` parameter that would have avoided this, and I didn't use it. The `sauce-solana-distributed-swap` skill, under \"Running it\" step 1, never mentions `limit` for the MCP route. It shows only the `jq` pipeline, which assumes the CLI and a saved `pools.json`. Neither the tool nor the skill warns that the default is unbounded, and the 44-entry `skipped` list is returned every time.\n\n2. **The pools come back ordered by liquidity, but the skill's rule is \"deepest *and cheapest* first\".** The tool sorts by liquidity. The Orca pool is deepest but not cheapest (117.75 against 117.71 for the 1-bp PancakeSwap pool). It came first in my slots, so it took the whole order. The tool and skill don't flag that liquidity order and price order differ, or say how to order slots when they disagree. The result was one pool filling everything. That is legal under the limit but not the best price, and it doesn't show a split across venues.\n\n3. **\"Every venue that supports a price limit\" can't be done in one cook.** The skill's own limit is about five pools, and the Solana pair has dozens. The skill says this under \"What fits in one cook\", but nothing in the tooling helps choose between venues or batch several cooks. I covered three venue families, not every one.\n\n4. **The skill is CLI-first, but I only had MCP tools.** `AGENTS.md` and the MCP instructions say there is no `sauce` command. The skill's steps are all `sauce pools/simulate/run`, `--accounts accounts.json` and `jq`. I had to translate each one by hand, and building `accounts.json` needed Bash and `jq` anyway. A tool that returns the `accounts` object for the top N pools would have removed this step.\n\n5. **I had to retype the 16 KB example by hand.** The skill says to use its code whole, but nothing copies a skill example to disk. `sauce_skill` only returns text, so I re-emitted all of it through a Write call. That risks transcription errors, and I didn't diff my file against the original. `sauce_install_skills` is named in the server instructions but wasn't among the tools I could load.\n\n6. **Minor.** The `sauce_pools` description says Solana sides are \"as above but for depth1Pct\", which is easy to misread, and `depth1Pct` is `null` for every pool. `sauce_simulate` returns long logs by default, and I didn't use its `summary` option. I ran the simulation only; I didn't call `sauce_compile` separately.\n\nThe skill's maths held up. The floor row (0.0084 gives `119047619047619047`) matched the task exactly, and the simulation succeeded on the first run."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":72578,"cache_read_input_tokens":12678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":72578},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d7dcf0e-4c18-4bb4-a012-a71fc371fcf5","uuid":"7811b7f0-df3c-493e-a2e8-0202cdb8b5a3","timestamp":"2026-10-07T10:38:14.276Z","request_id":"req_011CfnjNy2j7WPF6Cex6vJHT"}
